  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Gymnopilus sapineus, commonly known as the Scaly Rustgill, is a small woodland mushroom characterized by its rusty-orange coloring and distinctive scaly cap. This member of the Hymenogastraceae family is found throughout New England, typically fruiting in fall on or near coniferous wood.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Rusty-Orange Gills",
      "description": "The gills are a distinctive rusty-orange to rust-brown color, which is the source of the 'Rustgill' common name and helps differentiate it from similar species.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Scaly Cap Surface",
      "description": "The cap displays small, fibrous scales that give it a textured appearance, ranging from tan to reddish-brown in color.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size",
      "description": "This species is notably small, with caps typically 1-4 cm in diameter, making it easy to overlook among leaf litter.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Gymnopilus sapineus grows on or near decaying coniferous wood, particularly pine and spruce, in moist forest floors throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its small, inconspicuous appearance, Scaly Rustgill is believed to be psychoactive like some of its larger Gymnopilus relatives, though it is rarely consumed and remains relatively understudied.",
